			<div class="content" id="post-content-2400572">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Why would there even /be/ links to differing versions of the thing in what is apparently an actual database page?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The idea (which I admit, I couldn't really come up with a way to express directly, only by implication) is that everything here is protected by varying degrees of antimemetic camouflage. So most viewers aren't going to see anything in the directory. Memetics Department viewers can only see RED TALISMAN. Antimemetics can see RED TALISMAN and RIDDEN TONGUE. Counterconceptual can see RED TALISMAN and REVENANT THEORY. The dead can see everything.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why is the RED TALISMAN article not Thaumiel?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The same logic that makes <a href="/scp-148">SCP-148</a> Euclid?</p>
<p>This isn't being used directly to contain any other SCP objects but rather to generate the security glyphs for informational and physical security throughout the Foundation. If Thaumiels are the box in the locked box test, this is a thing that's in a box that they get the labels for other boxes from&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In RIDDEN TONGUE why would an antimeme thing create kill agents?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The files contain several layers of cognitohazardous, memetic, and antimemetic agents generated by the action of ghosts recording information.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The what?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="/scp-2358">SCP-2358</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>And… the antimeme team isn't allowed to know about it. Why?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Counterconceptual doesn't know that Antimemetic even exists.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I thought infovore was just a newbie's way of saying antimeme because they didn't know what an antimeme was.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>An antimeme is an idea that negates ideas. An infovore is an enitity that eats information. These are obviously different things&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You… Why would putting quotes around something magically make it immune to tone requirements?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Witch-hunt&quot; has a sociological meaning, and shows up in discussions of destructive uncontrolled mole-hunts in covert organizations. I suppose I could have used &quot;moral-panic&quot; but the former is more common in the covert world, I gather. If you can suggest an alternative, I'll consider it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Seriously what is the Counterconceptual Division? Why does it exist? What is its purpose?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Intelligence, counter-intelligence, research, development, and security involving antimemetic anomalies.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Ghosts have access to the Foundation database.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ghosts are <em>haunting</em> the Foudation database. That's basically the theme of this in a nutshell.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Wait now we have antimeme agents?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At least as long as <a href="/we-need-to-talk-about-fifty-five">We Need To Talk About Fifty-Five</a>, the Antimemetic Division was exploiting antimemes directly.</p>
<p>Just to be clear &quot;agent&quot; here means &quot;targeted memetic carrier&quot; not &quot;secret operative&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>…Did… did they die before 1981? Are you saying the Foundation actually talks to ghosts?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If they died before 1981 they can't remember anything before that date. The first thing any Omega-Zero operative remembers postmortem is logging into the database.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How is 'the astral plane' clinical?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As far as I know the term is in use in parapsychology. If it isn't I'll happily change it to one that does. Of the parapysch terminology in this article this is practically the only one I didn't invent&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is seriously just casually stating there are ghosts. Which I am one of, because I am reading this document.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Having the addressed reader be a fictional character is as old as epistolary narratives<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-109361-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-109361-1')">1</a></sup>. Every SCP article is addressed to fictional Foundation operatives, which you are not&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm guessing they're supposed to be responsible for orchestrating lies within the Foundation? Or something? But I don't know why the Foundation is lying to itself here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I used it more-or-less straight from the original appearance. Both Antimemetics and Counterconceptual have the same basic mission, and neither knows the other exists because both have invested heavily in antimemtic camouflage for their operations. The only conceit that's original to this piece is that Antimemetics is slightly better at intelligence and Counterconceptual is slightly better at counter-intel.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>EDIT: I feel like you missed a lot that was in the message from Amos Sanchez. Did you skip the collapsibles?</p>
