					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606401915" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1446422672 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Nov 2015 00:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2400698">
									

<p>There's a lot of elements in here that I normally do not like. I feel like this <em>just</em> escaped being too intentionally dense with the meme/antimeme business. The thing where an author throws a whole bunch of complicated logic-puzzly stuff is fast becoming a hallmark of this particular subject, much like it was with time fuckery when that was all the rage about a year ago or so. I would ask future writers in this particular sandbox to tread with caution, because it's a very fine line between genuine thought provocation and inking bullshit all over the place like some sort of bad writing squid.</p>
<p>I'm also not <em>generally</em> a fan of the &quot;well here guy let me explain things to you&quot; as a device. In this instance, I think it's mostly warranted given the subject matter, but it still grates a little bit every time I see it.</p>
<p>Those things said, the core concept was one that was cool enough that I finished reading and went &quot;hmmm, that was neat&quot;, and it felt enough like a story that it kept me interested enough to wade through some of the slower parts. Unlike a lot of other people, I enjoy the idea that ghosts are a real thing in the Foundationverse. And so of course, this being the case, one's service to the Foundation does not end with death.</p>
<p>Craftsmanship, preservation of immersion and just plain entertainment count for a lot with me, and so I enjoyed this. I can see where others may not have, but for me personally, it worked pretty well.</p>

